---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/apps/drip";
const title = "Drip for Shopify: evaluate SMS inside an existing email journey";
const desc = "Assess Drip SMS rollout eligibility, email-linked profiles, one-way toll-free sending, workflow exits and subscriber economics.";
const faqs = [{"question": "Is Drip email-only?", "answer": "No. Drip documents an SMS rollout for eligible US-based paid customers with SAP enabled. Confirm account access and approval before relying on it."}, {"question": "Can I collect SMS-only subscribers?", "answer": "Drip\u2019s current SMS page says a phone number requires an email and SMS-only subscribers are unsupported."}, {"question": "Can shoppers have a two-way SMS conversation?", "answer": "Drip describes its launch SMS as one-way toll-free sending. Provide a monitored support route instead of promising replies in the texting channel."}];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} dateModified="2026-11-25" />
  <article class="container py-16 prose-shopify">
    <a href="/apps">All app reviews</a>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <h2>Where Drip can help</h2>
    <p>Drip is worth evaluating when an existing email journey needs a timely text using the same audience and workflow context. That can reduce duplicate segmentation work. It still requires channel-specific eligibility and a tested dispatch decision. The exercises below are proposed acceptance tests, not merchant case studies or measured conversion improvements.</p>
    <h2>Current SMS availability</h2>
    <p>Checked November 25, 2026: <a href="https://www.drip.com/sms">Drip’s SMS page</a> describes phased access for existing US-based paid customers with Support All People (SAP) enabled. Each phone number requires an email; SMS-only subscribers are unsupported. Launch scope is one-way toll-free sending, without two-way texting, keyword-to-join or short codes. Confirm access in the account before planning a launch; do not describe Drip categorically as email-only.</p>
    <h2>Use the shared workflow deliberately</h2>
    <p>The <a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/48924286539661-Quick-Glance-SMS-with-Drip">SMS overview</a> documents targeted broadcasts and SMS workflow steps, plus activation review. A useful design sends product education by email and reserves SMS for a relevant time-sensitive event. First confirm the Shopify event and fields your connected workflow actually receives. A shared profile does not prove permission for both channels or cancellation of an already queued text.</p>
    <h2>Proposed identity fixture</h2>
    <p>Create an email-permitted shopper without SMS permission, a shopper with separate permission for both channels, and an attempted phone-only signup. Check the last case against the email prerequisite rather than inventing a placeholder address. Then change an email address, change a phone number and attempt duplicate imports. Record which person owns the number and the retained permission evidence. Pause an ambiguous identity instead of merging consent from different people.</p>
    <h2>Proposed cart workflow fixture</h2>
    <p>Start a cart journey with email education, a delay and an SMS decision. Place an order during the delay; in another run withdraw SMS permission; in a third leave the cart untouched. Save event arrival, decision and dispatch times, order/cart identifiers and message outcomes. The purchase should exit the relevant cart reminder, while a withdrawal changes only the permissions it actually covers. Verify email fallback separately so a failed text does not cause an unsolicited email or repeat the same offer.</p>
    <h2>Competing journeys and quiet hours</h2>
    <p>Schedule a broadcast while the cart fixture is waiting, then move the recipient across a quiet-hours boundary. Decide which eligible message has priority and whether a delayed offer expires before the next permitted window. Repeat with a second cart and a later purchase to expose stale events. Record any native control you used and any operator procedure needed to enforce the rule. Do not assume a shared workflow gives a global frequency cap across all campaigns.</p>
    <h2>Service expectations for one-way sending</h2>
    <p>Write SMS copy that routes order questions to an actively monitored support page or email. Test that link on mobile and assign an owner for failed delivery and customer confusion. Verify STOP handling with approved test recipients and inspect subsequent campaign eligibility. One-way sending must not be presented as a conversational support inbox. Keep essential service notifications outside marketing holdouts, and identify the system responsible for those messages before retiring an existing sender.</p>
    <h2>Model the two billing meters</h2>
    <p>Drip bills SMS separately from email. Its <a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/48926725054349-How-We-Calculate-SMS-Billing">SMS billing guide</a> describes subscriber and segment tiers with a high-watermark system. Build a worksheet with the email plan, active SMS audience, segments per campaign and workflow volume. Compare steady volume with a peak acquisition month and the following quieter month; verify how peak usage affects the invoice rather than assuming deleting contacts immediately lowers it. Preview personalized messages and encoding before estimating segments.</p>
    <h2>Measure the added text</h2>
    <p>For eligible dual-channel shoppers, compare the existing email journey against the same journey plus the SMS step, with random assignment before entry. Keep the offer and service access consistent and report net sales per assigned shopper over an agreed return window. Subtract goods, fulfillment, incremental send and support costs; do not subtract discounts or refunds twice if net sales already includes them. Report failed deliveries, exits and opt-outs with denominators. Platform attribution is useful diagnostic context, not proof of incremental lift.</p>
    <h2>Current-state decision</h2>
    <p>Proceed with a limited workflow only after account approval, identity mapping and dispatch exits pass. If the audience requires phone-only capture or conversational replies, the documented launch scope does not fit that requirement; preserve a suitable existing route while evaluating alternatives. If access is unavailable, keep email journeys working and revisit confirmed account access before setting an SMS launch date. Use <a href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">coordination</a> and <a href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a> to document ownership rather than buying a preset supplier stack.</p>
  </article>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
